so just click right on knit.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.070
What happens is that
this document gets turned

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.520
into an HTML file.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:58.120
So all of this text here
has gotten turned this file,

00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:58.953
which is really cool.

00:05:58.953 --> 00:06:01.970
It's a combination of text, of art code,

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:04.841
the output from the R code including plots

00:06:04.841 --> 00:06:07.840
and various other formatting options.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.140
So why is Markdown so awesome?

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:12.010
Well, first of all,

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.090
you can organize your
analyses much more clearly

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.280
for reproducibility.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.900
So for example here,
this is just an example

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:21.510
but you can have all your text explaining

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.600
how you did the analysis
with the actual code

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.400
that does the analysis and the output

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:27.620
and the output figures and results.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.310
So you can share what you
did with anyone that you want

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.040
to very easily and they can
see exactly what you did

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.030
and they can replicate it
themselves if they want.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.570
You also remain accountable
for your analysis,

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.740
because what you did is clearly visible

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.670
and you also can't cheat these outputs.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:46.130
In fact, the final knit
document is exactly

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:49.220
what the R code would give you
if you run this whole thing.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:52.590
So you can't just figure outputs
if someone runs this code,

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:55.023
they will get exactly what you got below.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.010
You can also create tutorials
to share with others,

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.050
hey, kinda like what I'm doing here

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.290
but also like the blog posts that I write,

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:06.190
they're all written in R
Markdown, and it's an easy way

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.610
to combine regular text as well as R code.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:15.610
And finally, you can use this
system to help you learn.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.890
So, I'm specifically going to
talk about how you can use it

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:22.720
for learning R, but essentially
it's a platform where...

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.620
It's a system where you can
enter in all your information,

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.260
all your notes about the learning process,

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.630
and then also include the actual functions

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.840
in code as you're learning

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.290
so that you can essentially
create this solid record

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.100
of what you were able to learn in the form

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:44.100
of this really neatly
polished file, HTML file

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:46.830
in this case that you can
then reference back again

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.220
whenever you need to remember
something that you learned

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.040
or want to go over the material
again to study for an exam

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.943
or just for learning R and
getting better at it over time.

