Now let's say we want us to
make this whole thing bold.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:31.060
All we have to do is put
those two asterisks before

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:35.830
and after, and this is not gonna work.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:37.480
No, look at that, it worked.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:39.290
I thought it was gonna turn purple, pink,

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:40.600
like that but it didn't.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.740
Either way look at that, it worked.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:47.740
So, you can also add
all sorts of HTML code

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:50.360
however you like to this document as well

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:53.490
but I'm going leave that
out of this conversation

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:56.760
'cause I wanna keep this as
simple as possible for you,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:58.880
but just mentioning that that's an option.

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:02.750
There's also a really cool
R Markdown cheat sheet made

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:05.510
by R studio which I'm going to link here

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.960
which has all these different
things that you can do,

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:09.270
all these different
formatting things you can do

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:12.600
on R Markdown, but don't
get overwhelmed with it,

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:15.480
my suggestion is don't even
worry about that for now.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.780
Just this is pretty much
all you need to know,

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:20.150
making a few different
headings bold, italics

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.870
and bullet points and lists,
it's pretty much all you need

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:24.730
for really creating really nice documents

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:28.140
of your R learning
process as you go along.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.090
So that's my suggestion,

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:32.180
don't get bogged down with too much more.

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:35.510
But now I'm gonna go into what R Markdown

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:37.340
is perhaps most famous for

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:40.380
because it's not just
Markdown, it's R Markdown.

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:44.500
And that is actually including
code chunks of our code.

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:46.320
So to do that, we're going to go up

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:47.370
and it's gonna make this a little smaller

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:48.430
so you can see this.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:51.993
There's this insert button
seat, insert button up here.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:54.760
If you click on this
little arrow, you can see,

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:57.280
you can insert all kinds
of other types of code

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.010
but we're again gonna
just leave the default

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.530
'cause we're not doing any
other programming right now,

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:02.540
we're just doing R.

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:05.330
So we're going to just click here,

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:07.180
wherever you wanna insert the R code,

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:09.120
click on this insert button,

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:12.270
Oh, I guess it pulls down
this menu no matter what.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:17.270
Click on R and here we have
what is called a code chunk.

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:19.620
And you could have also
typed this out by hand

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:21.650
but it's way easier to
just click that there.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:26.650
These are are not normal
quotes, single quotes,

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:30.620
they're the weird quote
symbol that's located

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:33.040
under this on a Mac keyboard, US keyboard,

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:37.923
it's located under the escape
key alongside the tilde,

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:42.263
this symbol if you can see that.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:45.050
Don't worry about typing this,

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:49.180
just click on the insert code
chunk and then inside here,

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:53.550
you can now type whatever
you want as R code.

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.853
So if I typed Hello World,

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:00.460
Worldphones, huh, interesting.

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:03.123
If I type Hello World and try to run this,

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:05.900
boom, we get an error.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:09.330
And that's because you can't
just type Hello world into R,

00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:10.720
you need to make it into a comment

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:13.100
so now we're gonna add
a pound hashtag there.

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:15.830
Keep in mind, this is not
the same thing as this here

00:17:15.830 --> 00:17:18.050
which tells you that
it's gonna turn the text

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:22.370
into a heading instead in R
when you use the pound sign

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:26.660
or the hashtag sign, whatever
you type after that hashtag

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:31.660
is commented out meaning it
is not run as actual R code,

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.430
it's run as a comment.

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:36.640
So now if we knit this,

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.740
take a look at our document
as it's coming along,

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:43.350
boom, here we go, we have
a little chunk of R code

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:44.860
and all we have in this R code

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:47.683
is a little comment, Hello World.

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:52.650
But let's push this, let's take
this a little further along.

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:56.163
So let's play around with some
numbers and plot some data.

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:02.400
We'll say, my code here,
just another little comment,

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:05.100
it's always good to
comment a lot in our code,

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:06.660
make it all very clear,

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:07.850
make it very clear what you're doing

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:10.980
and the cool thing about R
Markdown is that you don't have

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:13.680
to put tons of comments
inside the actual code bit,

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:16.140
you can write everything
you want around before

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:18.300
and after the code chunk to re...

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:19.910
And you can go into much more detail

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:21.670
and make the formatting
all fancy and everything

00:18:21.670 --> 00:18:24.880
so that it's really clear
what this R chunk of code

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:25.900
is gonna be doing.

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:28.760
All right, so let's say...

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:31.780
And lets create this variable
answer and into answer,

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:34.383
we're gonna put two plus four.

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:38.420
Then let's see what answer looks like,

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:42.600
we'll just run answer on
its own to see the result.

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:46.840
Then we'll create another
one, my data and we'll use

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:50.710
the car's data which is
actually already uploaded

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:54.000
into R when you start the new session.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.520
You just won't see it in your environment

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:59.840
but it's there so this
should work even though

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:03.310
we haven't actually uploaded
this data set separately,

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:04.820
it's already there.

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:08.960
And let's take a look at
what my data looks like.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:13.960
So now, if we knit this, scroll down,

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:19.610
we can see that the code
chunk has actually been split

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:22.520
into every code chunk that has an output.

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:26.660
So here is where we wrote
an answer is two plus four,

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:29.150
put that into the variable answer.

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:30.900
And then when you run
just answer on its own,

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:31.950
it creates an output.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:34.290
So now we have the second box under here

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:36.260
that shows you the output.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:38.650
So these hashtags here just indicate

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:41.210
that it's the console output.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:43.590
The one in brackets here just indicates

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:46.180
that it's just row one of whatever else.

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:49.240
If you had tons of numbers
here and it created a new row

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:51.740
and wrapped around, you'd
have a one and then a two

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:53.050
and then a three and so on.

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.180
So you can ignore everything

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:56.950
that's highlighted right now there.

00:19:56.950 --> 00:19:58.820
The only important thing is the six,

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:01.560
because two plus four
is six, so there you go.

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:05.580
Then the other part of the chunk
of code was my data putting

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:07.800
in this car's data set into my data

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:12.490
and when you run my data,
it shows you this dataset

00:20:12.490 --> 00:20:14.550
of cars data, not really
sure exactly what it is

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:15.990
but it looks like one column is speed,

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:20.220
one column is distance and
somehow those are related

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.730
and mean something about cars.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:25.180
So, that's all there is to it.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:29.370
You can also as you're working
through the code yourself

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:30.720
so that you don't have to knit

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:32.650
and see everything go each time

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:34.910
and then if you have an
error like let's say,

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.560
I forgot to type that R there
answer and I try to knit this,

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:41.120
instead of this knitting
and showing me what's wrong,

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:43.890
I'm just gonna get this error down here

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:46.579
which is actually harder
to read or understand

00:20:46.579 --> 00:20:48.370
than actually if you were going

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:49.940
through the code line by line,

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:52.520
the way that I suggest in my tutorials.

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:54.330
So what you can do is actually go

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:56.240
through the code line by line.

00:20:56.240 --> 00:21:00.210
And the way you do that is
the same as using R script,

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.410
you click the cursor wherever you want

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:07.410
and then you hold command
on a Mac or control on a PC.

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:12.960
And you hit return on
a Mac or enter on a PC.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.570
And as you run through it,
you will see the output,

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:17.590
there was no output with
Hello World and it kinda

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:19.870
just skipped through this
one and then this one,

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:22.553
it went all the way down
to the first line of code

00:21:22.553 --> 00:21:24.420
that actually did something.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:26.940
And so when that line of code runs,

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.600
nothing actually happens
because there's no output.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:31.880
But then when we run
this next line of code,

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:36.880
boom, we get this error
object answer, not found.

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:39.240
That's an error, let's
put that R back in there.

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:42.370
Now, when we run that line,
there you go we have the output.

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:44.990
And then we keep running the
lines of code, we run that one.

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:46.150
Again, there's no output because

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:48.380
we're just putting this
cars data set directly

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:50.360
into this new variable, my data.

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:52.110
But then when we run my data,

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:57.110
we get this nice, neat
little output of data.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:02.210
So, that way we can walk
through and bug check the code

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:04.540
to make sure that it works
and now that it's working,

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:06.602
we can go back and click knit

00:22:06.602 --> 00:22:10.293
and it will come up and work nicely here.

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:14.210
Now let's create another chunk of code.

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:17.010
We'll say, just in the Markdown,

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:22.010
here we're just gonna say,
here is my next chunk of code.

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:27.090
This is how you use the plot function

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:36.190
to plot a scatter plot to let's say

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:37.973
to create a scatter plot.

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:44.190
And the other cool thing is in R Markdown,

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:48.840
you can use that weird, not
really a single quote thing

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.480
that I mentioned before that's used here

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:53.490
to create these code chunks.

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.460
Again, it's located under the
escape key on the left side

00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:00.263
of the keyboard on a
Mac American keyboard,

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.560
also on the same key as the tilde,

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.360
the little squiggly thing.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:08.170
So if you use those quotes,
those types of quotes

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:10.820
and you add one around...

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:14.810
Well, you add them around a chunk of text,

00:23:14.810 --> 00:23:17.220
it'll turn blue or some
other color depending

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:18.500
on your color scheme here.

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:23.500
And that turns this into
a little code snippet.

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:26.830
So instead of it plotting
with the regular font

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:29.760
that gets output in the HTML document,

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:34.500
it'll turn it into a fixed width
font similar to the R code.

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:36.030
So maybe it's better to just show you

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:37.680
rather than trying to explain it.

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:42.640
So here, I don't know if you can see it,

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:45.253
but maybe I can make this really big.

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:50.670
So, here's my next chunk of code,

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:53.220
this is how you use the plot function.

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:55.770
So you can see plot is kind of circled

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:58.340
by this like gray box and
now it's written in the same,

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:01.680
fixed with font is as code
so it's a little code snippet

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:03.800
just to make it clear that this is a piece

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:05.440
of code you're referring to.

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:06.273
You don't have to do that,

00:24:06.273 --> 00:24:08.840
it's just another quick little
formatting tweak you can do

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:13.193
and use an R Markdown small again.

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:16.270
Can I make this small again?

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:17.103
There we go.

00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:23.460
So now let's create our next chunk of code

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:28.240
and we're gonna say plot cars$speed

00:24:31.172 --> 00:24:34.180
is a function of cars$dist.

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:39.780
So for anyone totally new to R,

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:43.000
basically what I'm doing
here is I'm using this cars,

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:44.350
actually I don't even have to call it cars

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:47.377
I can call it my_data because
I already saved my_data

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:50.223
as its own data frame.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:52.850
I already saved the cars data set

00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:54.940
as my own data frame called my_data.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:58.040
And the dollar sign tells
me select the vector

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:00.410
in this data frame called speed.

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:02.430
So if we were to run, select that,

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:06.040
and then do command +
enter or control + enter,

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:08.150
you can see that it's just this vector

00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:10.150
of values under the speed column.

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:11.720
And then this is the vector of values

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:13.610
under the distance column.

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:16.530
And we're saying plot this speed column

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:21.530
as a function of this distance column.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:26.680
And essentially that's saying
the X variable on the X-axis

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:33.270
on this plot will be a function
of the Y-axis on this plot.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:35.383
So if we run this,

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:39.280
we get this nice little scatter plot here.

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:42.533
And if we were to run the
whole thing using knit,

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.620
you can see that this
using the plot function

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:49.657
also created this little
scatter plot here.

00:25:49.657 --> 00:25:53.320
That's important to note
that like a regular R script,

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:56.360
you do have to run all the
code chunks one after another

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:59.800
if certain variables or packages
that you need lower down

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:02.910
in the code are located
somewhere higher up in the code.

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:06.750
So for example, here
I'm referring to my data

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.640
and I only define my data here.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:12.540
So if I were to restart R, so
I'm just gonna go to session,

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:17.540
Restart R and Clear Output,
I just restarted everything.

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.360
Now you can see that the
environment is empty.

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:26.258
So, we created this variable my_data

00:26:26.258 --> 00:26:28.470
from this car's data frame.

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:31.320
So if we were to try to run
this piece of code here,

00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:34.023
you'll see if there's an
error, my data not found.

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:38.410
And so even though we click
to knit the whole thing

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:39.930
and it works,

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.820
it works because when
knitting the whole thing,

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:44.660
it runs all the code
from beginning to end.

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:47.550
So, even though it ran everything already

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:52.180
through the knitter function,
when we actually go in

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:56.120
and want to view the code
line by line or run this chunk

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.640
of code, you can click this green button

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:01.160
or just run it line by line like that.

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:04.090
You'll still get this error
because in the environment,

00:27:04.090 --> 00:27:06.470
there's no variable called my data yet

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:07.830
because it wasn't saved there.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:09.510
So you actually have to go in here

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:11.070
and run this chunk of code first,

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:12.910
and then this chunk of code to make sure

00:27:12.910 --> 00:27:14.580
that it's all in the environment.

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:17.217
You can also, let's say we wanna run this.

00:27:17.217 --> 00:27:21.480
And we know let's say our R
Markdown script is really long,

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:22.330
there's all this other stuff,

00:27:22.330 --> 00:27:24.470
we don't know where we define my data,

00:27:24.470 --> 00:27:26.670
but we know that it's
somewhere previous to this,

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:28.811
you can also click this button here

00:27:28.811 --> 00:27:33.520
which if you hover over it,
it says, run all chunks above.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:38.300
So this will run everything
up until this chunk of code.

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:41.490
And look now in the environment,
the my_data data frame

00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:45.010
is now present so now if
we run this line of code,

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:47.440
it will create this figure again.

