00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:05.293
it's a really, really powerful tool.

00:33:06.600 --> 00:33:10.120
I'm gonna end this section
with a really quick pointer

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:12.100
that I think is really
valuable and important

00:33:12.100 --> 00:33:15.310
to add to all of your
R Markdown documents.

00:33:15.310 --> 00:33:17.320
This is something that
I'm definitely guilty

00:33:17.320 --> 00:33:20.370
of not always doing but I
really, really recommend it,

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:24.100
adding a code chunk at the end
of your R Markdown document

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:29.100
with the function session,
and then with a capital I info

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:31.943
and then just two parentheses.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:35.693
So if we run this,

00:33:37.460 --> 00:33:41.320
we get this output that
shows us the version of R

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:42.570
that we're working with,

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:45.640
the platform I'm using an Apple computer,

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:49.210
I'm running Mac OS Big Sur.

00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:52.010
And then a bunch of other
information about the packages

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:54.060
that I have installed and that I'm using.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:58.133
The reason this is important
so if we knit this,

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:05.000
we can even add a little
title here, Session Info.

00:34:10.910 --> 00:34:13.150
The reason this is important at the end

00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:14.670
of every R Markdown document

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:17.710
is that sometimes things change.

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:19.610
So are upgrades in certain versions,

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:22.390
certain packages upgrade their versions

00:34:22.390 --> 00:34:26.670
and certain things might
not work again the same way

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:29.240
in the future when you go back
and you find this tutorial,

00:34:29.240 --> 00:34:31.620
you find this sheet that
you made and you try

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:34.150
to enter the code as it
is and you get an error.

00:34:34.150 --> 00:34:36.440
And that might be because the new version

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:39.070
that you're using isn't
completely compatible

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:41.750
with everything that
you created this sheet

00:34:41.750 --> 00:34:42.860
with in the past.

00:34:42.860 --> 00:34:45.900
And so there's ways to
download older versions of R,

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:47.550
there's ways of downloading older versions

00:34:47.550 --> 00:34:51.370
of different packages so that
you can make it compatible.

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:55.100
But at least that way you might
know why there's an error,

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:57.710
why something's not working
exactly as you had planned.

00:34:57.710 --> 00:35:00.470
And so like I said this
is especially important

00:35:00.470 --> 00:35:02.950
when you want to share
your R Markdown files

00:35:02.950 --> 00:35:06.550
with other people just
to make sure they know

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:08.100
what system you are running to make sure

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:11.445
that everything functions
properly but especially...

00:35:11.445 --> 00:35:13.730
Excuse me, that's especially important

00:35:13.730 --> 00:35:15.830
but then when you're
using it for yourself,

00:35:15.830 --> 00:35:17.930
for your own purposes, for your own use,

00:35:17.930 --> 00:35:20.880
it's still kinda useful
to include just in case

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:22.450
you come back to something after a while

00:35:22.450 --> 00:35:23.730
and things have upgraded and changed,

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:25.360
you don't know why you're getting an error

00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:26.470
and it might be because of that.

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:28.930
So, really simple to add at the end

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:33.930
of your R Markdown files, but
just wanted to slip that in,

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:35.700
something important to do.

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:37.350
And that's it.

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:40.200
So next, I want to show you

00:35:41.260 --> 00:35:44.820
how you can use R Markdown to learn R.

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:46.660
And actually pretty
much this whole tutorial

00:35:46.660 --> 00:35:48.530
has been showing you that very thing,

00:35:48.530 --> 00:35:52.210
I'm just gonna cover a few
extra details because the point

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:56.850
is the end goal is to have a
series of R Markdown documents

00:35:56.850 --> 00:35:59.760
that you can use as a
reference for all the topics

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:02.260
and code that you learned.

00:36:02.260 --> 00:36:04.900
So this converts learning
R from pure practice

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:07.080
and memorization to really more

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:09.690
of a project based learning environment.

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:13.450
And since R Markdown is really
simple and straightforward

00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:15.480
to use basically just a text editor,

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:17.870
you can start using it
without really knowing much

00:36:17.870 --> 00:36:19.120
about R at all.

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:20.480
And as you progress,

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:22.920
you can document your
entire experience beginning

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:25.370
with something as simple
as how to define a variable

00:36:25.370 --> 00:36:28.030
and then moving onto more advanced things.

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:31.550
You basically create this
whole document that you have

00:36:31.550 --> 00:36:33.790
as a reference in case
you forget something,

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:35.380
need to look something up.

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:38.520
And then by creating it,
it's also forcing you

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:40.043
to learn the material better

00:36:40.043 --> 00:36:42.768
because you're essentially teaching it.

00:36:42.768 --> 00:36:45.570
So when you rewrite
things in your own words,

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:47.270
you're creating something,

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:49.610
you're creating a teaching
resource that others can use

00:36:49.610 --> 00:36:51.110
and you can use in the future.

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:53.840
And through that teaching
process, you're also going

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:56.650
to be learning the material
much better as well.

00:36:56.650 --> 00:36:57.483
So how do you do it?

00:36:57.483 --> 00:36:59.540
What are the important steps?

00:36:59.540 --> 00:37:02.470
Well, first create a new R Studio project.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:07.470
So we're gonna go to file,
new project, save that.

00:37:11.310 --> 00:37:12.860
We're not gonna use
version control for now

00:37:12.860 --> 00:37:16.850
just a new directory in
your computer, new project.

00:37:16.850 --> 00:37:21.850
Let's save this in my R tutorials folder,

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:24.610
we're gonna save this in the...

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:27.583
Actually I created an R
Markdown folder in here,

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:34.220
and we'll call this Learning R Tutorial.

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:41.423
Create the project.

00:37:42.830 --> 00:37:47.830
Now, the reason for using R
Studio projects is multifold,

00:37:48.430 --> 00:37:49.670
there's a lot of reasons.

00:37:49.670 --> 00:37:51.300
Long story short, I'm
not gonna go into this

00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:54.550
but create a new R project,
it'll just keep everything nice

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:55.760
and neat and organized.

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:57.930
And then whenever you want to
go back and continue adding

00:37:57.930 --> 00:38:01.750
to this, just go in and open
this learning in this case,

00:38:01.750 --> 00:38:03.740
it's called Learning R Tutorial.Rproj.

00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:07.030
But whatever your .Ropj file is,

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:10.750
just open that and it will open
this whole new session here.

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:14.520
Then we're going to go
to file new R markdown

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:19.200
and we'll call this The Basics.

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:22.050
So we're gonna create first
one that's called The Basics.

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:26.960
And the key is to create
separate R Markdown documents

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:30.510
as you go through each of the
major sections of learning R.

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:33.010
So for example, I created
one that's called The Basics,

00:38:33.010 --> 00:38:34.020
after you're done with that,

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:36.420
maybe you'll create one that's
called data visualization,

00:38:36.420 --> 00:38:38.640
one that's all about uploading data.

00:38:38.640 --> 00:38:40.340
One that's about data wrangling.

00:38:40.340 --> 00:38:44.010
So for example in the course
that I offer The Basics

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:47.210
of R for Ecologists, the way
that that course is broken down

00:38:47.210 --> 00:38:50.480
is into these major modules
that cover these general themes.

00:38:50.480 --> 00:38:52.150
And so within each of those modules,

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:55.460
you can create one R
Markdown document to try

00:38:55.460 --> 00:38:58.580
to cover as much as you
can within that section,

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:00.920
or you might want to break
it down even further.

00:39:00.920 --> 00:39:03.600
I wouldn't recommend including everything

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:06.070
in just one file, just 'cause it'll turn

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:08.150
into this really huge R Markdown document,

00:39:08.150 --> 00:39:09.580
you have to scroll through
it and all this stuff

00:39:09.580 --> 00:39:11.280
so better to keep it kinda organized

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:14.040
in these nice, neat sections.

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:18.620
Alternatively, you could do
one big R Markdown document

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:21.300
with a clickable table of contents

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:24.300
if you wanna be really fancy
and then you can easily access

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:26.990
the different sections
in the final document.

00:39:26.990 --> 00:39:29.900
So that is another alternative.

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:32.680
But the point is you want
to break it down somewhat

00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:35.310
even within the same document
with different sections of...

