00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:09.070
because it won't knit the whole document

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:10.440
when it stumbles upon an error.

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:12.410
So if we run this just line by line to see

00:41:12.410 --> 00:41:15.940
what the issue is,
unexpected symbol and this...

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:17.490
The point is this is not R code,

00:41:17.490 --> 00:41:21.140
we need to comment this out for
it to work without an error.

00:41:21.140 --> 00:41:22.550
But let's say this is some kind of...

00:41:22.550 --> 00:41:25.757
Let's make a different kind
of error, let's say my_data...

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:30.560
Let's see is my data greater...

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:33.792
My variable, my_number,
it's called my_number.

00:41:33.792 --> 00:41:36.180
Is my_number greater than five.

00:41:36.180 --> 00:41:39.000
We knit this, we still get an error

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:41.330
because if we run this line by line

00:41:41.330 --> 00:41:43.990
we'll see the error message,
object my_number not found,

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:45.560
we haven't defined my_number yet.

00:41:45.560 --> 00:41:49.040
But let's say you don't know
what that error really means.

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:53.743
What you can do is you can
copy this, paste it in here.

00:41:54.700 --> 00:41:57.230
We don't want that, we
just want the error.

00:41:57.230 --> 00:41:58.313
Comment it out.

00:41:59.190 --> 00:42:02.163
Comment out the line
that created the error.

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:08.490
And now when you knit,
you still have that R code

00:42:08.770 --> 00:42:12.770
but now you have what the
error was a description

00:42:12.770 --> 00:42:15.600
of the error plus the line
that 'caused the error.

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:17.850
So now if you share this
document with someone,

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:20.190
be like, look, I try all these things,

00:42:20.190 --> 00:42:21.700
this is what I'm typing,

00:42:21.700 --> 00:42:23.480
this is the error that I'm getting.

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:25.210
And then someone else could be like,

00:42:25.210 --> 00:42:27.680
oh, look, you didn't define my number yet,

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:32.680
you need to go in and say
my_number is six let's say.

00:42:34.570 --> 00:42:37.510
And now there shouldn't
be an error anymore.

00:42:37.510 --> 00:42:39.423
Now, if we run that, we run that.

00:42:40.560 --> 00:42:42.600
My number is greater than five, true.

00:42:42.600 --> 00:42:44.870
And now if we knit the whole thing,

00:42:44.870 --> 00:42:47.820
you see that it works, it
comes out, we have this output.

00:42:47.820 --> 00:42:50.420
And we can get rid of
this error message here.

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:53.290
So that way you can go
along and keep practicing

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:58.080
and trying different things as you go.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:42:59.560
Oh, and something I didn't show you

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:01.670
is that you can actually
for the table of contents,

00:43:01.670 --> 00:43:04.110
so here we have the basics
'cause this is the first section

00:43:04.110 --> 00:43:06.110
we don't have any other sections.

00:43:06.110 --> 00:43:08.500
When you go into the output options here,

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:11.690
you can number section headings.

00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:13.320
So this will add numbers to the section

00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:15.780
so you can actually follow
along like chapter numbers.

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:20.780
So now if we knit, you'll see
that we have one The Basics.

00:43:21.540 --> 00:43:23.260
And you'll see this one was added in front

00:43:23.260 --> 00:43:25.620
of The Basics that we know that it's that.

00:43:25.620 --> 00:43:28.990
And let's say we had a subsection on...

00:43:28.990 --> 00:43:31.110
So we'll call this heading two.

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:36.110
We'll use heading two for
this, finding variables.

00:43:37.570 --> 00:43:42.123
And we'll have another section
on vectors let's just say.

00:43:43.180 --> 00:43:47.130
So if we run this, now you
can see we have The Basics

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:49.370
and within The Basics we have section 1.1

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:52.350
to find variables and
section 1.2 on vectors.

00:43:52.350 --> 00:43:53.960
And you can see that these added

00:43:53.960 --> 00:43:56.280
into the table of contents as well.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:58.280
So that's it really?

00:43:58.280 --> 00:44:01.610
And basically just fill out the document

00:44:01.610 --> 00:44:03.250
as you're going through
the different lessons

00:44:03.250 --> 00:44:05.360
whether it's my course
or any other tutorials

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:07.970
or courses online, fill
out these documents,

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:12.430
create them, save them,
print them out even,

00:44:12.430 --> 00:44:16.170
go back and use them whenever
you need to as a refresher,

00:44:16.170 --> 00:44:18.330
but even just the
process of creating them,

00:44:18.330 --> 00:44:20.300
even if you don't ever look at them again,

00:44:20.300 --> 00:44:24.050
it really will help you be
able to grasp these topics

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:27.730
a lot more because it turns
learning R into a project

00:44:27.730 --> 00:44:30.800
where you're actually creating
this really cool resource

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:34.330
that you could eventually even
share it with other people.

